= Read the story

Priya had always wanted a garden, but the flat she shared with her grandmother had only one small window ledge. Every morning she carried a chipped blue jar to school in her backpack, and every afternoon she checked it again.

Inside the jar was a single sunflower seed. Her grandmother had given it to her on the day they moved in. "Seeds are patient," Grandma said. "They wait until they are ready to grow."

Priya did not feel patient. She watered the jar every day, even though the soil looked dry. She read books about plants and drew pictures of a tall yellow flower. Her classmate Tomas laughed at her. "Nothing is happening," he said.

One Saturday, a thunderstorm rattled the windows. When Priya woke up, she noticed a small green shoot poking through the soil. It was no taller than her little finger. She ran to the kitchen, where Grandma was making tea.

"It's growing!" Priya shouted.

Grandma smiled and set down her cup. "Now you must give it light and water, and then wait a little longer."

Over the next weeks, the shoot grew into a sturdy stem with two broad leaves. Priya moved the jar to the window every morning so the plant could catch the sun. Tomas came to see it one afternoon and was surprised at how tall it had become.

"Maybe I should start a garden too," he said quietly.

Priya grinned. "Seeds are patient," she reminded him.

